      {
        "id": "p11-q1-a",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Use the results from the previous exercise to write down the derivative of the following.",
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 3,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p11-q1-a-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "$f(x)=x$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$f'(x)=1$",
              "worked": "The function $f(x)=x$ is a straight line with gradient $1$, so $f'(x)=1$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 10
          },
          {
            "id": "p11-q1-a-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "$f(x)=x^2$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$f'(x)=2x$",
              "worked": "Using the derivative result for $x^2$, $f'(x)=2x$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 10
          },
          {
            "id": "p11-q1-a-iii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "iii",
            "prompt": "$f(x)=x^3$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$f'(x)=3x^2$",
              "worked": "Using the derivative result for $x^3$, $f'(x)=3x^2$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, hypothesise a formula for the derivative of $f(x)=x^n$, where $n$ is a positive integer.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=nx^{n-1}$",
          "worked": "In part (a), the original exponent becomes the coefficient and the exponent decreases by $1$. This suggests $$\\frac{d}{dx}(x^n)=nx^{n-1}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      }
    ]
